<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Environment and Recycling – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Environment and Recycling</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> In the Past, People Didn't Recycle as Much as They Do Now.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Infer implicit information when listening to current events. Interactive: Respond to questions in class discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can infer simple details from short talks about sustainable living by recognizing key cause-effect relationships (e.g., "What happens if we waste water?" "We have less to drink.")<br>Can respond to questions about recycling during class discussions, demonstrating understanding of main ideas (e.g., "What should we recycle?" "Cans, paper, cardboard, batteries, and plastic containers.")<br>Can listen to peers discussing water conservation, showing comprehension by recognizing specific suggestions (e.g., "Use less water when washing dishes.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 40%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 45%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows two large pictures: one of a dirty landfill overflowing with waste from "the past" (e.g., old cars, non-recyclable items), and another of a modern recycling facility or clean park, representing "today." Students are asked to briefly describe what they see. The teacher prompts with questions like: "What do you see in the first picture?" "What about the second one?" "Do you think people recycled in the past?" "Do people recycle today?" This activates prior knowledge and introduces the theme. The teacher writes key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>waste, recycle, environment, pollution</i>) on the board as students mention them or as the teacher introduces them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the pictures and uses target grammar: "In the past, people didn't recycle that much." and "Today, people are more aware of the importance of recycling." The teacher models the pronunciation of core vocabulary (<i>recycle, waste, environment, pollution, energy, landfill, planet, resource, reduce, reuse, clean, save, protect</i>) by saying each word clearly and having students repeat chorally. The teacher specifically models the stress contrasts between present and past tense statements, focusing on verb endings, e.g., "We <i>watch</i>" vs. "We <i>watched</i>." Then, models the "-ed" endings: /d/ in "saved," /ɪd/ in "wanted," and /t/ in "watched," providing clear examples.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the concept and new vocabulary.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I throw all my plastic bottles in the regular trash, am I recycling?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'landfill' a good place for our planet?" (Expected answer: "No, it's bad for the environment.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio clip (1.5-2 minutes) from a news report or a simple dialogue between two teenagers discussing "The Environment and Recycling." The audio focuses on past versus present recycling habits and general environmental practices. It incorporates target grammar (past simple vs. present practices, first conditional, modal 'should') and vocabulary (<i>recycle, waste, environment, pollution, energy, landfill, planet, resource, reduce, reuse, clean, save, protect</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can infer simple details from short talks about sustainable living and recognize key cause-effect relationships. The student can also respond to questions about recycling during class discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (First Listen - Gist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher tells students to listen for the main idea. Play the audio once.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the audio mainly about? A) How to cook healthy food, B) How the environment has changed, C) How to play a game." (Expected answer: B)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listen - Specific Details):</b> The teacher plays the audio again. Students listen and mark True or False for simple statements written on the board or a handout, e.g.:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In the past, people often recycled plastic bottles." (False)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The audio suggests we should save water." (True)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If we waste too much, our planet will be clean." (False - This implicitly checks understanding of the first conditional for cause-effect.)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific details, using past simple vs. present practices, first conditional, and modal 'should', while reinforcing vocabulary related to the environment and recycling. They also practice distinguishing past tense "-ed" endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listening Cloze):</b> The teacher provides a transcript of a section of the audio with blanks for key vocabulary (e.g., <i>recycle, waste, pollution</i>) and verbs in the past simple (e.g., <i>didn't recycle, saved</i>). Students listen to the audio one more time and fill in the blanks.
                            <br>Example: "In the past, people ____ (not recycle) paper. They just threw ____ (waste) away. Today, we ____ (should) protect our ____ (environment)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Substitution Drill (Listen and Repeat with Pronunciation Focus):</b> The teacher says sentences, and students listen and repeat, paying attention to the pronunciation of past tense "-ed" endings and stress. The teacher then provides a prompt, and students listen and form a new sentence.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "We saved water." (Students repeat, focusing on /d/ sound.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "In the past, they watched a lot of TV." (Students repeat, focusing on /t/ sound.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "She wanted to recycle." (Students repeat, focusing on /ɪd/ sound.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "If we waste plastic, it pollutes the ocean." (Students repeat.)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher Prompt: "If we waste water..." (Student listens and completes: "...there will be less for everyone.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher Prompt: "You should turn off the tap when..." (Student listens and completes: "...brushing your teeth.")</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check of Comprehension):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives each pair a set of cards with questions related to the audio's content (e.g., "What did people do with old newspapers in the past?", "What should we recycle today?", "What happens if we waste energy?"). One student reads a question, and the other listens and responds, demonstrating their comprehension and use of target vocabulary and grammar. They take turns asking and answering.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Discussion and Problem-Solving.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher divides the class into small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a specific "environmental problem" scenario on a card (e.g., "Our community has a lot of plastic waste and no recycling bins," "People often leave the water running in our school," "There is too much pollution in our local river"). Students listen to the problem presented to their group. Then, they discuss and propose solutions, focusing on:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Contrasting past and present practices: "In the past, people didn't care about plastic, but now we should..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Giving advice: "We should put more recycling bins." "You should turn off the tap."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Expressing cause-effect: "If we recycle more, there will be less pollution." "If we waste water, there will be less for everyone."</li>
                    </ul>
                    They must use the target vocabulary (<i>recycle, reduce, reuse, waste, environment, save, protect, pollution</i>) in their discussion. The teacher circulates, listening to their interactions and offering support. Finally, each group briefly reports one or two of their solutions to the class, requiring other students to listen to their peers discussing environmental issues and recognizing specific suggestions.
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative Assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the group discussion and reporting phase.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group discussion, listening to the problem and their peers' contributions, and then presenting their group's solutions. The teacher observes their ability to understand and respond to the task, use target language, and collaborate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of the environmental problem (listening comprehension).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>recycle, waste, environment, protect, save</i>) appropriately.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies target grammar (past vs. present, first conditional, 'should') correctly in context.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of past tense "-ed" endings is mostly intelligible.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates effectively in the group and responds to peers' ideas.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned ______ new words about the environment."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I should do to help the environment is ______."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to the environmental discussion was easiest for me? What was most challenging?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use the words 'reduce, reuse, recycle' in my daily life?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially concerning students' listening comprehension of new vocabulary and grammar? (e.g., the audio material was engaging, the CCQs were effective, students actively participated in the group discussions).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to reinforce listening skills and vocabulary? (e.g., provide more visual aids for vocabulary, offer sentence starters for responses, dedicate more time to '-ed' pronunciation practice).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a short text about environmental efforts in different communities, applying the same vocabulary and contrasting past and present practices. This will reinforce their understanding of the words heard today and prepare them for reading comprehension using similar themes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>